
{"id":"1d26b50d-098b-40f2-bba2-d524f128f04d","object":"chat.completion","created":1784983748,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\\n        parent = n['parent']\\n        if parent is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n        else:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    count = {sym: 0 for sym in required_symbols}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            count[n['symbol']] += 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    status = {}\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            status[nid] = 'retained'\\n            continue\\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained_child = any(status.get(cid) == 'retained' for cid in children)\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            status[nid] = 'retained'\\n        else:\\n            if n['removable']:\\n                sym = n['symbol']\\n                if sym not in required_symbols or count.get(sym, 0) > 1:\\n                    status[nid] = 'removed'\\n                    removed_order.append(nid)\\n                    if sym in required_symbols:\\n                        count[sym] -= 1\\n                else:\\n                    status[nid] = 'retained'\\n            else:\\n                status[nid] = 'retained'\\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid, s in status.items() if s == 'retained'])\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` based on the provided instructions and fixtures. The task is \"SE-PE-01\", described as \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The payload contains a tree of nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The goal: reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols and respecting constraints.\n\nFrom the output schema: return `removed_node_ids` (order of removal) and `retained_node_ids` (sorted IDs of nodes that are kept). Need to decide which nodes to remove.\n\nInstructions: \n- Consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes.\n- Never remove root, required symbol nodes, or nodes with retained children.\n- Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\n- Use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth.\n- Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nGiven the example payloads: nodes have tree with root depth 0, children depth 1, etc. removable flag indicates if node is candidate for removal. However, even if removable, removal must satisfy constraints: required_symbols must still be present in the tree after removal (i.e., the symbol must appear in some remaining node). Also, a node cannot be removed if it has children that are retained (i.e., if we remove a node, all its descendants are also gone; so we must ensure that we don't remove a node that is needed to hold children we want to keep). It says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means if a node is removed, its entire subtree is gone. So we should only remove a node if we are okay with losing all its children. Typically, we want to remove entire branches that are not necessary.\n\nGoal: Reduce tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols. This is reminiscent of Perses tree reduction: remove nodes that do not affect interestingness (here, required symbols presence). Since there's no external interestingness oracle, we interpret \"interestingness_preserved\" as \"required symbols remain present\".\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build tree structure from nodes list. Each node has id, parent (null for root), depth, removable, symbol.\n- Build parent-child relationships. Also map symbol to nodes that have that symbol.\n- Required symbols: set of symbols that must appear in retained nodes.\n- We want to iteratively remove removable nodes, starting from deepest nodes first (post-order). For each node, check if removal would cause loss of any required symbol: i.e., if after removal, the set of symbols in the remaining tree still contains all required symbols. But because we remove entire subtree, we must check if any required symbol is only present in that subtree (including node itself). Also, we must ensure that node is removable (flag true) and not the root. Also, node cannot be removed if it has children that are retained; but if we remove a node, all its children are removed too, so we shouldn't remove if some children are not removable? Actually the rule \"a node with retained children\" means if after our process we decide to keep some children, we can't remove the parent. So we need to process bottom-up: first decide for each node whether to remove it, based on its removable flag and required symbol coverage. But if we remove a node, its children are automatically removed; so we should only remove a node if all its descendants are also removable (or we are okay removing them). Since we are going bottom-up, we can consider removing a node if all its children have already been removed (or are removable). But the rule says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if we intend to retain any child (e.g., because it's necessary for required symbol or not removable), we cannot remove the parent. So bottom-up: we decide for each node (starting from leaves) whether to keep or remove. If a node is kept, its ancestors cannot be removed (since they have retained children). If a node is removed, we also remove its subtree (but that subtree's nodes are already decided? We need to be consistent: a removal decision is final; we cannot remove a node and then keep some of its children. So in bottom-up, we process each node after its children decisions are made. For node, if it has any child that is retained (not removed), then node cannot be removed (even if removable). If all children are removed (or node has no children), then we can consider removing it if it's removable and its removal does not cause loss of required symbols (i.e., the required symbols are still present in other parts of the tree). Also root cannot be removed (even if removable flag? root has removable:false in fixtures). So we need to compute, for each node, the subtree's contribution to required symbols. We can compute a set of \"required symbols covered by subtree\" for each node. Initially, each node contributes its own symbol if that symbol is in required_symbols. But required symbols can appear multiple times; we only care that at least one occurrence remains after removal.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build adjacency: parent -> children list, and node dict.\n- Identify required_symbols set.\n- For each node, compute initially a set of required symbols present in its subtree (including itself). This can be done bottom-up DFS.\n- Also need to know if a node is \"essential\": i.e., if removing it would cause loss of a required symbol that is not present elsewhere in the tree (outside its subtree). But since we are removing entire subtree, we need to know if the symbol is present outside. This is like a global check. A simpler approach: we process nodes in post-order (deepest first). For each node, if it is removable and not root, and all its children have been removed (i.e., no retained children), then we check if the required symbols that are present in its subtree are still present at least once elsewhere in the tree (including in nodes that we have already decided to retain). Initially, all nodes are considered retained. We simulate removal: if we decide to remove node, we mark it removed and update some global set of \"available required symbols\". But since we process post-order, we can maintain a set of retained nodes and count of each required symbol in retained nodes. Initially, all nodes are retained, compute counts. Then iterate nodes in post-order. For each node that is removable (and not root) and all children are already removed? But children processed earlier: if a child is retained, we cannot remove parent. So we need to know after processing children which are removed/retained. So we can do DFS post-order: when we visit node after children, we have decisions for children. Then we decide for node: if any child is retained, node cannot be removed (must be retained). If all children are removed (or no children), then node is candidate for removal. If candidate, we check if removing node would cause any required symbol count to drop to zero. That is, for each symbol in required_symbols that appears in node's subtree, we need to ensure that after removal, there is at least one other occurrence outside node's subtree that is retained. Since we have not yet removed node, we can compute total required symbol counts and subtract the count within node's subtree. But careful: some of the symbols in node's subtree might also be present in other parts that are already removed? But we are only removing nodes that are removable and whose removal doesn't cause loss. So we need to maintain dynamic counts as we remove nodes. However, given that we process in post-order, the removal decisions for ancestors have not yet been made. So if we remove a node, its descendants are already removed (or this node itself will be removed, so its subtree will be gone). So we can treat removal of node as also removing its entire subtree, which includes children that may have already been removed. But children are either removed or retained; if retained, node cannot be removed. So case of children removed: they are already marked removed. So when we remove node, we are effectively also \"removing\" (already removed) children. So the required symbol counts that need to be decremented are those present in node's subtree, including node and its descendants that are currently retained? But if descendants are already removed, they are not counted in retained counts. So we need to know, when evaluating removal of node, what is the current count of each required symbol in the set of nodes that are still retained (including node and any children that are still retained? But if children are retained, node cannot be removed). So if all children are removed, then the only nodes in subtree that are still retained are potentially just the node itself (since children removed). So the required symbols present in node's subtree that are currently retained are only those in node itself (if not yet removed). Plus maybe some descendant retained? But we said if children removed, they are not retained. So it's just node. So we can simply check: if node's symbol is a required symbol, and there is no other retained node (outside this subtree) that also has that symbol, then removal would cause loss. So we need to maintain a map from required symbol to count of retained nodes that have that symbol. Initially, we have all nodes retained. We'll process nodes in post-order. For each node, we consider its children: if any child is retained, node must be retained (because it has retained children). So we mark node as retained and proceed. If all children are removed, then we check if node is removable. If removable, we check if the required symbol of node (if any) has count > 1 (i.e., there is at least one other retained node outside this node's subtree with that symbol). But note: the count of retained nodes with that symbol includes node itself currently. So we need count > 1. However, also the node's subtree might contain multiple occurrences of same required symbol from descendants that are already removed. But those are not retained, so they don't affect count. So effectively, for node's removal, the only potential loss is the symbol of node itself (if it is a required symbol). What about required symbols that appear only in descendants but not in node? Those descendants are already removed; so if we haven't removed them, they would have been considered earlier. But if they are removed already, then they are gone, and we already ensured that removal didn't cause loss. So it's safe. So the check reduces to: if node is removable and all children are removed, then we can remove it if (node.symbol not in required_symbols) or (count_of_required_symbol[node.symbol] > 1). Because if node's symbol is required, we need at least one other retained node with that symbol.\n\nBut there is also the possibility that a required symbol appears only in this node and its descendants (which are already removed). Then count_of_required_symbol would be 1 (only this node). Removing would cause zero, so we must not remove. So condition holds.\n\nEdge case: node has no children, same logic.\n\nNow, we need to maintain counts dynamically as we remove nodes. So we process in post-order, and when we decide to remove a node, we decrement the count for its symbol (if required) by 1. When we decide to retain a node, we keep count as is.\n\nBut note: the count should reflect the number of retained nodes (excluding those already removed) that have each required symbol. Initially, count is total nodes with that symbol. As we remove nodes, we decrement. So during processing, when we check removal condition for node, we need the current count (which includes this node if it hasn't been removed yet). Since we are about to possibly remove it, we check if count > 1. But careful: if node's children were removed earlier, they already decremented the count (if their symbols were required). So the current count is accurate.\n\nSo algorithm:\n1. Parse nodes into dict by id.\n2. Build children list for each node (parent -> children).\n3. Identify root: node with parent null.\n4. Compute required_symbols set.\n5. Initialize count_of_required = {symbol:0 for sym in required_symbols}\n6. For each node, if node.symbol in required_symbols: count_of_required[node.symbol] += 1.\n7. Perform post-order traversal (DFS) to get list of nodes in order of depth descending (or just do recursion and process after children).\n8. For each node in post-order (children before parent):\n   - Determine if any child is retained. We need to track retention decisions. Use a set retained_ids, initially all ids? But we will decide. Better to have a dict `status` per node: 'retained' or 'removed'. Initially none.\n   - For root, always retain (cannot remove, even if removable? root removable false anyway).\n   - For other nodes:\n     - Check children: if any child has status 'retained' (or still undecided? But we process bottom-up, so children are decided before parent. So children status is known. If any child retained, then node must be retained (can't remove because has retained children). Set status='retained'.\n     - Else (all children removed or no children):\n       - If node is removable (node['removable'] == True) and node['id'] != root_id:\n         - Check removal condition: if node['symbol'] not in required_symbols or count_of_required[node['symbol']] > 1:\n           - Then we can remove this node: set status='removed', decrement count_of_required for node['symbol'] if required.\n         - else: must retain (cannot remove because would lose required symbol). Set status='retained'.\n       - else: not removable or root, so must retain. Set status='retained'.\n\n9. After processing all nodes, we have retained_ids and removed_ids (order of removal). The removal order should be the order in which we marked nodes as removed during post-order traversal (i.e., deeper nodes first, then parents). We'll collect removed_node_ids in the order we decide to remove.\n\n10. retained_node_ids sorted lexicographically (or by original order? specify \"sorted retained IDs\" – probably alphabetical/numerical sort by string id). The fixtures show ids like \"n1\",\"n2\", etc. So sort them as strings.\n\n11. Return { \"removed_node_ids\": list of removed ids in removal order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list of retained ids }.\n\nTest with first fixture:\n\nnodes:\n- root (depth0, removable false, symbol root)\n- n1 (depth1, parent root, removable false, symbol s1)\n- n2 (depth1, parent root, removable true, symbol s2)\n- n3 (depth1, parent root, removable false, symbol s0)\n- n4 (depth2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1)\n- n5 (depth2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2)\n- n6 (depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0)\n\nrequired_symbols: root, s1.\n\nCounts initially: root symbol 'root' count 1 (root). s1 symbol count: n1 and n4 => 2.\n\nPost-order: depths: depth2 leaves: n4, n5, n6; depth1: n1, n2, n3; depth0: root.\n\nProcess n4: parent n2, removable true. children? none (or none). All children removed (trivially). Check removal: symbol s1 is required, count=2 >1, so can remove? Yes. Remove n4. Decrement count of s1 to 1. removed list: ['n4'].\nn5: parent n3, removable false. Not removable -> retain. status retained. no change.\nn6: parent n1, removable true. symbol s0 not required -> remove. Decrement none. removed list ['n4','n6'].\nNow depth1:\nn1: children: n6 was removed, so no retained children. n1 is not removable (removable false) -> retain.\nn2: children: n4 was removed. no retained children. n2 is removable true. Symbol s2 not required -> remove. Decrement none. removed list ['n4','n6','n2'].\nn3: children: n5 is retained (since not removable). So n3 has retained child -> must retain.\nroot: children: n1, n2 (removed), n3 (retained). So root has retained children (n1 and n3) -> must retain.\n\nFinal retained: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root']? Actually root is 'root'. Sorted strings: 'n1','n3','n5','root' (since 'n' comes before 'r'? 'n' ASCII 110, 'r' 114, so yes). Check required symbols: root and s1: root and n1 (s1) present. OK.\n\nNow check if this matches expected? Not given. But we need to ensure rule: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" – we followed that. Also we considered deeper first. Tie break: at equal depth, use node ID as stable tie break. Our post-order traversal should respect depth and at same depth, order by id? The order within same depth doesn't matter as long as we process all children before parent. But if multiple nodes at same depth, they are independent. However, if there are dependencies (like sibling), order might affect outcome because removal of one might affect count for another? In this problem, they are independent because each node's subtree is separate (except parent-child). Since we process after children, siblings don't affect each other because counts are global. However, if two siblings share a required symbol and both are candidates, order of removal among them could affect whether the second can be removed: after removing first, count drops, so second might not be removable. So tie break matters. Instruction: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That means when there are multiple nodes at same depth (same depth value) that could be processed in any order, we should sort them by node ID to get deterministic order. In post-order, we need to ensure that for nodes at same depth, we process them in sorted order of IDs (or by some rule). Since children are processed first, the order among children of same parent? The depth is not the only factor: children may have different depths. But for nodes that are at the same depth but not siblings, they are independent subtrees. The traversal order should be deterministic. I'll implement a post-order that sorts children by ID before recursing, so that processing order is deterministic. Also, when constructing the list of nodes in post-order, we can gather them and then sort by (depth, id) but post-order naturally goes depth first; but to enforce tie break at equal depth, we need that among nodes with same depth, we process in fixed order. Since we sort children by ID, then within a subtree, nodes of same depth (e.g., leaves under different parents) will appear in the order determined by the recursion, but it might not be strictly sorted by global ID across the whole tree. However, the requirement \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\" likely means that when deciding between multiple identical-depth candidates (in the same recursion level, i.e., same parent?), we break ties by ID. But it's safer to implement a priority-based processing: for each node, we want to consider removal in order of decreasing depth, and for equal depth, lower ID (or higher?) \"use node ID as the stable tie break\" - could be ascending order. In typical tie-breaking, smaller ID first. I'll assume ascending (lower ID first). But the problem states \"tie break at equal depth\" – so if two nodes have same depth, we order them by ID (probably string comparison). So we should process them in that order. To achieve that, we can generate a list of all nodes, sort by (-depth, id) (so deepest first, then ID ascending) and then process in that order, but we also need to ensure that children are processed before parents. If we sort by depth descending, then all depth 2 come before depth1, ensuring children before parents. However, we also need to check that when we process a node, its children have already been processed (i.e., we have decided their status). Since all children have greater depth, they will appear earlier in the sorted list, so yes, they are processed before parent. So we can simply sort all nodes by depth descending (larger depth first) and then by id ascending. Then iterate. That satisfies: deeper nodes first, same depth sorted by id. This is simpler than recursion. We must ensure that for a node, its children have been processed already. Since children have depth = parent depth + 1, they have larger depth, so they appear earlier. So we can use this list.\n\nThus algorithm steps:\n- nodes list from payload.\n- Build dict node_by_id.\n- Build children mapping.\n- Determine root id.\n- required_symbols set.\n- Initialize count_of_required as dict with counts from all nodes (initially all retained).\n- Create list of node ids sorted by (-depth, id). \n- status dict: initially None.\n- removed_order = [].\n- For each node_id in sorted list:\n   - node = node_by_id[node_id]\n   - if node_id == root_id: status = 'retained' (skip removal).\n   - else:\n        # Check if any child retained\n        children = node_by_id[node_id].get('children', []) # we populated earlier\n        has_retained_child = any(status.get(cid) == 'retained' for cid in children)\n        if has_retained_child:\n            status[node_id] = 'retained'\n        else:\n            # All children removed (or no children)\n            if node['removable']:\n                # Check removal condition\n                symbol = node['symbol']\n                if symbol not in required_symbols or count_of_required.get(symbol,0) > 1:\n                    # Remove\n                    status[node_id] = 'removed'\n                    removed_order.append(node_id)\n                    if symbol in required_symbols:\n                        count_of_required[symbol] -= 1\n                else:\n                    status[node_id] = 'retained'\n            else:\n                status[node_id] = 'retained'\n- After loop, retained_ids = [nid for nid, s in status.items() if s == 'retained'] (or could get from nodes where not removed).\n- Sort retained_ids (string sort) and return.\n\nCheck with example: Should produce removed_order: first depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Sorted by id: n4, n5, n6. For n4: removable, children none, count s1>1 -> remove. n5: not removable -> retain. n6: removable, symbol s0 not required -> remove. Then depth1: n1, n2, n3 sorted: n1, n2, n3. n1: removable false -> retain. n2: children n4 removed, no retained child -> removable true, symbol s2 not required -> remove. n3: children n5 retained -> must retain. Then root: will be processed last. root has retained children n1 and n3 -> retain. So removed_order = ['n4','n6','n2'] as before. Retained sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root'].\n\nNow need to verify with other fixtures; similar logic.\n\nEdge cases: If a required symbol appears only in a node that is removable and all its children are removed, and that node's removal would drop count to 0, we retain it. Good.\n\nAlso need to treat nodes that are not removable but might have removable children? Already handled: if child is retained, parent can't be removed even if removable. If child removed, parent may be removable if condition holds.\n\nPotential issue: What if a node has a child that is removable and we decided to remove that child, but later we decide to keep the parent? That's fine. But if parent is kept, the child is already removed, so that's okay.\n\nWhat about a node that has a child that is not removable but we decided to keep it? Then parent can't be removed.\n\nNow write solve(case) function. case is a dict with 'nodes' list and 'required_symbols' list. Also may have '_case_nonce' ignored.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Ensure not to mutate input; we create local structures.\n- Use standard library only.\n\nNow produce output with \"implementation\" key containing Python code as a JSON string. Careful with escaping.\n\nI'll produce code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    \n    node_by_id = {}\n    children_map = {}  # parent_id -> list of child ids\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\n        parent = n['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n        else:\n            root_id = nid\n    \n    # Add children list to each node for convenience\n    for n in nodes:\n        n['children'] = children_map.get(n['id'], [])\n    \n    # Initialize counts of required symbols\n    count = {sym: 0 for sym in required_symbols}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            count[n['symbol']] += 1\n    \n    # Sort nodes: by depth descending, then by id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    status = {}\n    removed_order = []\n    \n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid == root_id:\n            status[nid] = 'retained'\n            continue\n        \n        children = n['children']\n        has_retained_child = any(status.get(cid) == 'retained' for cid in children)\n        if has_retained_child:\n            status[nid] = 'retained'\n        else:\n            if n['removable']:\n                sym = n['symbol']\n                if sym not in required_symbols or count.get(sym, 0) > 1:\n                    # remove\n                    status[nid] = 'removed'\n                    removed_order.append(nid)\n                    if sym in required_symbols:\n                        count[sym] -= 1\n                else:\n                    status[nid] = 'retained'\n            else:\n                status[nid] = 'retained'\n    \n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid, s in status.items() if s == 'retained'])\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n```\n\nBut need to ensure root_id is defined even if no explicit parent=null? The fixtures have root with parent null, so fine.\n\nPotential bug: When checking 'removable' field, the payloads have boolean values. Use n['removable'] directly.\n\nAlso, need to handle case where removable node has no children: children list empty, so has_retained_child false.\n\nWhat about a node that is removable but has children that are all removed? Already covered.\n\nNow ensure